ied-release capsule without assuming the same rule for Omnic OCAS tablets?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Chew the capsule before the evening meal","explanation":"Chewing can disrupt its modified-release design."},{"id":"B","text":"Take after the first meal, swallowing the capsule whole","explanation":"The referenced capsule is taken after breakfast or the first meal and swallowed intact."},{"id":"C","text":"Use OCAS directions without checking the package","explanation":"Different tamsulosin product forms can have different administration instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"Open the capsule and mix its contents into food","explanation":"The referenced modified-release capsule should be swallowed whole."}],"point":"Distinguish Omnic modified-release capsule meal instructions from OCAS product instructions.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--comprehensive--tamsulosin_capsule_meal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_capsule_meal"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8,"correctionRevision":"formulation-quality-audit-20261106","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"omnic-product-2026","title":"AEMPS Omnic 0.4 mg modified-release capsules SmPC","url":"https://cima.aemps.es/cima/dochtml/ft/61426/FT_61426.html"},{"id":"dailymed-tamsulosin-2026","title":"DailyMed tamsulosin hydrochloride capsules","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=db6cb771-2cff-40b7-a5ef-ea3cf2a3b461"},{"id":"eau-urolithiasis-2026","title":"EAU 2026 Urolithiasis Guidelines","url":"https://uroweb.org/guidelines/urolithiasis/chapter/guidelines"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker).","mechanism":"Antagonizes alpha-1A and alpha-1D adrenergic receptors, reducing smooth-muscle tone in the prostate and urethra. In the ureter, alpha-blockade may relax smooth muscle and help selected stones pass.","use":"Relieves lower urinary tract symptoms due to benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), such as weak urinary stream, hesitancy and incomplete emptying. In an ED, alpha-blockers including tamsulosin may also be prescribed OFF-LABEL as medical expulsive therapy (MET) for selected uncomplicated distal ureteral stones, particularly 5–10 mm, after clinician assessment. Not a treatment for sepsis or an infected obstructed urinary system.","administration":"ORAL only: not an IV or IM medication. For the usual Omnic 0.4 mg modified-release capsule, official product labeling states once daily after breakfast or the first meal, swallowed whole without crushing or chewing. Do NOT assume Omnic OCAS prolonged-release tablets have identical meal instructions: check the exact product and prescription. If interrupted for several days, review the restarting instruction in the actual product's labeling/order.","monitor":"• Before giving: assess blood pressure, recent dizziness, falls or fainting; alpha blockade can cause orthostatic hypotension/syncope, especially when starting treatment or with other BP-lowering medicines. • Reassess positional dizziness and safe mobility; teach the patient to sit/lie down if faint. • Verify any other alpha-blocker, PDE-5 inhibitor (sildenafil/tadalafil), or strong CYP3A4 inhibitor because combinations can increase hypotension/exposure. • Ask about planned cataract/glaucoma surgery: tamsulosin is associated with intraoperative floppy iris syndrome (IFIS), and ophthalmology should know even about past use. • In a suspected ureteral stone, fever/sepsis, anuria, deteriorating renal function, or uncontrolled pain are red flags: notify the medical team urgently; medical expulsive therapy alone is inappropriate in infected obstruction.","why":"Selective alpha-1 receptor blockade relaxes smooth muscle in the prostate, bladder neck and distal urinary tract, easing urinary flow and, in suitable patients, facilitating passage of distal ureteral stones. Systemic alpha blockade can lower standing blood pressure; the iris effect is clinically important before eye surgery.","availability":"Reported by learner as an Emergency Department medication on 3 Nov 2026. Product/strength and actual shelf stock have not been independently photographed or verified. Typical Omnic modified-release capsules are 0.4 mg; Omnic OCAS is a different prolonged-release tablet presentation. Check the physical pack before use."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker).","id":"section-0-c232b3398f02a2f1"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Antagonizes alpha-1A and alpha-1D adrenergic receptors, reducing smooth-muscle tone in the prostate and urethra. In the ureter, alpha-blockade may relax smooth muscle and help selected stones pass.","id":"section-1-d65eb30bce98cd2e"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Relieves lower urinary tract symptoms due to benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), such as weak urinary stream, hesitancy and incomplete emptying. In an ED, alpha-blockers including tamsulosin may also be prescribed OFF-LABEL as medical expulsive therapy (MET) for selected uncomplicated distal ureteral stones, particularly 5–10 mm, after clinician assessment. Not a treatment for sepsis or an infected obstructed urinary system.","id":"section-2-6465d24f34d85305"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"ORAL only: not an IV or IM medication. For the usual Omnic 0.4 mg modified-release capsule, official product labeling states once daily after breakfast or the first meal, swallowed whole without crushing or chewing. Do NOT assume Omnic OCAS prolonged-release tablets have identical meal instructions: check the exact product and prescription. If interrupted for several days, review the restarting instruction in the actual product's labeling/order.","id":"section-3-f4d922269516314d"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Before giving: assess blood pressure, recent dizziness, falls or fainting; alpha blockade can cause orthostatic hypotension/syncope, especially when starting treatment or with other BP-lowering medicines. • Reassess positional dizziness and safe mobility; teach the patient to sit/lie down if faint. • Verify any other alpha-blocker, PDE-5 inhibitor (sildenafil/tadalafil), or strong CYP3A4 inhibitor because combinations can increase hypotension/exposure. • Ask about planned cataract/glaucoma surgery: tamsulosin is associated with intraoperative floppy iris syndrome (IFIS), and ophthalmology should know even about past use. • In a suspected ureteral stone, fever/sepsis, anuria, deteriorating renal function, or uncontrolled pain are red flags: notify the medical team urgently; medical expulsive therapy alone is inappropriate in infected obstruction.","id":"section-4-b63a3223935db8b8"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Selective alpha-1 receptor blockade relaxes smooth muscle in the prostate, bladder neck and distal urinary tract, easing urinary flow and, in suitable patients, facilitating passage of distal ureteral stones. Systemic alpha blockade can lower standing blood pressure; the iris effect is clinically important before eye surgery.","id":"section-5-caf3ed7b380c4f70"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"Reported by learner as an Emergency Department medication on 3 Nov 2026. Product/strength and actual shelf stock have not been independently photographed or verified. Typical Omnic modified-release capsules are 0.4 mg; Omnic OCAS is a different prolonged-release tablet presentation. Check the physical pack before use.","id":"section-6-3788ec8170bd287a"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard","itemId":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin","itemType":"medication","title":"OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alpha-1A/alpha-1D antagonism reduces bladder-neck and prostatic smooth-muscle tone, easing urinary flow."},{"id":"B","text":"Relaxes prostate and urethral smooth muscle","explanation":"Alpha-1A/alpha-1D antagonism reduces bladder-neck and prostatic smooth-muscle tone, easing urinary flow."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduces vagal bronchoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alpha-1A/alpha-1D antagonism reduces bladder-neck and prostatic smooth-muscle tone, easing urinary flow."},{"id":"D","text":"Increases renal glucose and sodium excretion","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alpha-1A/alpha-1D antagonism reduces bladder-neck and prostatic smooth-muscle tone, easing urinary flow."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Alpha/beta blocker","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker)."},{"id":"B","text":"Dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker)."},{"id":"C","text":"Uroselective alpha-1 blocker","explanation":"Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker)."},{"id":"D","text":"Non-dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Selective alpha-1A/alpha-1D adrenergic receptor blocker (uroselective alpha-blocker)."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relief of lower urinary tract symptoms caused by benign prostatic hyperplasia","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Relieves lower urinary tract symptoms due to benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), such as weak urinary stream, hesitancy and incomplete emptying. In an ED, alpha-blockers including tamsulosin may also be prescribed OFF-LABEL as medical expulsive therapy (MET) for selected uncomplicated distal ureteral stones, particularly 5–10 mm, after clinician assessment. Not a treatment for sepsis or an infected obstructed urinary system."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of circulating glucose in severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute reversal of opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral). Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"B","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."},{"id":"D","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::hypotension"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient taking OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) reports dizziness on standing. What is the priority nursing assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Blood glucose alone","explanation":"Glucose does not assess the suspected drug-related positional pressure drop."},{"id":"B","text":"Respiratory rate at rest only","explanation":"This does not directly assess orthostatic symptoms."},{"id":"C","text":"Daily temperature trend","explanation":"Temperature does not establish postural hemodynamic effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Sitting and standing BP with symptoms","explanation":"Alpha-1 blockade may cause orthostatic hypotension; a safe posture and BP/symptom reassessment help identify risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Prevent postural hypotension, syncope and falls with alpha-1-blocker therapy.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--tamsulosin_orthostasis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_orthostasis"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient taking OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) is scheduled for cataract surgery. Which information is important to communicate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Current or past tamsulosin exposure","explanation":"Tamsulosin is linked with intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether the patient uses paracetamol","explanation":"This is not the main medication-specific concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Timing of the patient's last meal only","explanation":"Fasting requirements do not address iris effects."},{"id":"D","text":"History of seasonal allergies only","explanation":"This does not address the drug-specific operative issue."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Notify the eye surgeon about current or previous tamsulosin use before cataract surgery.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--tamsulosin_ifis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_ifis"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Besides benign prostate symptoms, what is a selected emergency-department use of OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dissolving bladder infection bacteria","explanation":"Tamsulosin is not an antibiotic."},{"id":"B","text":"Stopping gastrointestinal hemorrhage","explanation":"It has no hemostatic action."},{"id":"C","text":"Helping passage of distal ureteral stones","explanation":"Medical expulsive therapy is an off-label use for carefully selected distal ureteral stones."},{"id":"D","text":"Rapidly relieving pulmonary edema","explanation":"It is not a diuretic or pulmonary-edema rescue medication."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize selected distal ureteric stones as an off-label ED use of tamsulosin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--tamsulosin_distal_stone","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_distal_stone"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient prescribed OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) for a ureteral stone has fever and signs of sepsis. What is the safest nursing response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Encourage ambulation as the only intervention","explanation":"Mobility does not address a urological sepsis emergency."},{"id":"B","text":"Rely on stone-expulsive therapy","explanation":"Alpha blockers do not treat infection or decompress an obstructed system."},{"id":"C","text":"Wait for the stone to pass before calling","explanation":"Delay in infected obstruction can cause rapid deterioration."},{"id":"D","text":"Urgently escalate suspected infected obstruction","explanation":"Sepsis with an obstructed kidney requires prompt resuscitation, antibiotics and urologic decompression."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize that infected urinary obstruction is an emergency and not managed by tamsulosin alone.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--tamsulosin_infected_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_infected_obstruction"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What instruction applies to the taught Omnic 0.4-mg modified-release capsule without assuming the same rule for Omnic OCAS tablets?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Chew the capsule before the evening meal","explanation":"Chewing can disrupt its modified-release design."},{"id":"B","text":"Take after the first meal, swallowing the capsule whole","explanation":"The referenced capsule is taken after breakfast or the first meal and swallowed intact."},{"id":"C","text":"Use OCAS directions without checking the package","explanation":"Different tamsulosin product forms can have different administration instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"Open the capsule and mix its contents into food","explanation":"The referenced modified-release capsule should be swallowed whole."}],"point":"Distinguish Omnic modified-release capsule meal instructions from OCAS product instructions.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--standard--tamsulosin_capsule_meal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_capsule_meal"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":9},{"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced","itemId":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin","itemType":"medication","title":"OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient taking OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) has poor intake and a new BP fall when standing. Which factor best explains increased sensitivity to treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced gastrointestinal transit","explanation":"Slower transit does not account for the positional hemodynamic change."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased vascular resistance","explanation":"This generally supports pressure rather than explaining the described fall."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased circulating volume","explanation":"This does not explain the volume-depleted presentation."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced circulating volume","explanation":"Volume depletion can amplify symptomatic hypotension."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::hypotension"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"The prescribed OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"B","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"C","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."},{"id":"D","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::formulation"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient starts OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) and becomes faint while walking to the bathroom. What should the nurse do first?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Give the next dose immediately","explanation":"The next dose is not the immediate response to faintness."},{"id":"B","text":"Increase fluid intake without assessment","explanation":"Fluid may be relevant but cannot replace immediate safety assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Assist to a safe position and assess BP","explanation":"Support the patient and assess for symptomatic hypotension before further therapy."},{"id":"D","text":"Encourage independent walking","explanation":"Walking unaided risks fall injury with syncope."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Prevent postural hypotension, syncope and falls with alpha-1-blocker therapy.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--tamsulosin_orthostasis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_orthostasis"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient stopped OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) months before planned cataract surgery. What should the nurse still ensure?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Restarting the alpha blocker now","explanation":"Restarting treatment does not prevent operative iris problems."},{"id":"B","text":"The ophthalmologist knows the prior treatment","explanation":"Past exposure can remain clinically relevant to cataract surgery planning."},{"id":"C","text":"Changing to an injectable route","explanation":"Tamsulosin is an oral drug and this would not address the issue."},{"id":"D","text":"No disclosure because treatment ended","explanation":"IFIS has been reported with prior use."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Notify the eye surgeon about current or previous tamsulosin use before cataract surgery.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--tamsulosin_ifis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_ifis"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"For which ureteral-stone presentation does guidance most support an alpha blocker like OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An uncomplicated distal stone of 5–10 mm","explanation":"EAU 2026 particularly supports alpha blockers for distal ureteral stones 5–10 mm when conservative management is appropriate."},{"id":"B","text":"Bilateral obstruction with renal impairment","explanation":"Renal impairment with obstruction demands urgent evaluation."},{"id":"C","text":"Uncontrolled pain despite analgesia","explanation":"Uncontrolled pain requires escalation, not expulsive therapy alone."},{"id":"D","text":"Sepsis with obstructed infected kidney","explanation":"Infected obstruction requires urgent decompression and antibiotics."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize selected distal ureteric stones as an off-label ED use of tamsulosin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--tamsulosin_distal_stone","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_distal_stone"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient with an obstructing ureteral stone receiving OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral) develops anuria and worsening renal function. What changes the plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Using the medicine as immediate analgesia","explanation":"Tamsulosin is not a rapid pain-relief agent."},{"id":"B","text":"The need for urgent obstruction evaluation","explanation":"New anuria and renal deterioration are urgent warning signs, not indications for MET alone."},{"id":"C","text":"Increasing dosage without review","explanation":"Dose escalation does not resolve dangerous urinary obstruction."},{"id":"D","text":"Continuing unchanged home therapy","explanation":"Worsening obstruction or renal failure requires escalation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize that infected urinary obstruction is an emergency and not managed by tamsulosin alone.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--advanced--tamsulosin_infected_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ed-omnic-tamsulosin::tamsulosin_infected_obstruction"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin--7-day","itemId":"ed-omnic-tamsulosin","itemType":"medication","title":"OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"comprehensive memory retest; questions may repeat","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of OMNIC — tamsulosin (oral)?","ch