nt safety."}],"point":"Avoid routine IM injections during IV alteplase to reduce injection-site bleeding.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_avoid_im","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_avoid_im"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A second IV medicine is ordered while Activase is infusing. Which administration approach complies with Activase guidance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Mix into Activase after reducing infusion speed","explanation":"A slower rate does not make an unapproved admixture safe."},{"id":"B","text":"Add it to Activase after inspecting for particles","explanation":"Visual inspection cannot establish compatibility with Activase."},{"id":"C","text":"Administer separately without mixing into Activase","explanation":"Other medications should not be added to an Activase infusion."},{"id":"D","text":"Give through the line without checking the setup","explanation":"Medication line compatibility and the Activase infusion restriction must be respected."}],"point":"Do not add another medicine to the Activase infusion.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_no_mixing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_no_mixing"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"During Alteplase (tPA) therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."},{"id":"D","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Before alteplase for a suspected ischemic stroke, which completed assessment is essential to the eligibility decision?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Brain imaging excluding hemorrhage with protocol eligibility review","explanation":"Stroke symptoms alone do not establish suitability for thrombolysis."},{"id":"B","text":"A neurological score establishing the severity of deficits","explanation":"Severity assessment is needed, but it does not exclude intracranial hemorrhage or establish complete eligibility."},{"id":"C","text":"A symptom-onset time within the treatment window","explanation":"Timing is needed, but does not replace brain imaging and the full eligibility assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"A blood-pressure reading within the protocol limits","explanation":"BP is part of screening; an acceptable reading does not exclude hemorrhage or satisfy all eligibility criteria."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Exclude intracranial hemorrhage and verify eligibility before stroke thrombolysis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_screen","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_screen"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During alteplase, sudden severe headache, vomiting, and neurological worsening occur. Which complication is the priority to exclude?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Intracranial hemorrhage","explanation":"This is a major thrombolysis complication requiring immediate escalation and emergency assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"A routine migraine episode","explanation":"The treatment context and acute neurological change require urgent exclusion of hemorrhage rather than assuming a benign headache."},{"id":"C","text":"An uncomplicated therapeutic response","explanation":"Neurological deterioration is not a sign of successful treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"An isolated local infusion reaction","explanation":"A local site problem does not explain this neurological cluster."}],"point":"Acute neurological worsening during thrombolysis needs immediate escalation.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_neuro","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_neuro"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"The Activase label states a 3-hour stroke window, while current AHA/ASA guidance supports selected eligible patients within 4.5 hours. What must guide a time-sensitive treatment decision?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The assumption that any later presentation automatically qualifies","explanation":"Later or unknown-onset cases require specific selection criteria."},{"id":"B","text":"The assumption that symptom severity alone establishes eligibility","explanation":"Time, imaging, contraindications, and the protocol remain relevant."},{"id":"C","text":"The assumption that a treatment window excludes imaging requirements","explanation":"Hemorrhage exclusion and eligibility still matter."},{"id":"D","text":"The newest applicable authorized stroke protocol and eligibility assessment","explanation":"The label/guideline distinction must be recognized rather than mixed into an improvised rule."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stroke thrombolysis timing follows current eligibility guidance and local protocol.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_timing"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"A hypothetical 110-kg adult meets the stated stroke protocol eligibility. Using 0.9 mg/kg with a 90-mg maximum, what total dose is calculated? Enter mg.","answer":90,"unit":"mg","tolerance":0,"explanation":"110 × 0.9 = 99 mg before the cap; the maximum limits the total to 90 mg.","point":"Apply the taught 0.9 mg/kg stroke dose with 90 mg maximum.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_total"]},{"stem":"Which BP limits apply immediately before and during the first 24 hours after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Before: <160/90; afterward: ≤160/90 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the protocol limits."},{"id":"B","text":"Before: <185/110; afterward: ≤180/105 mm Hg","explanation":"These are the pre- and post-treatment limits."},{"id":"C","text":"Before: <200/120; afterward: ≤200/120 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the limits."},{"id":"D","text":"Before: <180/105; afterward: ≤185/110 mm Hg","explanation":"This reverses the two thresholds."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distinguish pre-treatment BP eligibility from post-thrombolysis limits.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_bp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_bp"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"An antiplatelet or anticoagulant is proposed after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke. What is required before starting it?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The authorized post-thrombolysis assessment and follow-up imaging plan","explanation":"Bleeding risk and follow-up findings guide the protocol decision."},{"id":"B","text":"A normal platelet count with no visible bleeding","explanation":"These findings do not replace the authorized post-thrombolysis assessment and imaging requirements."},{"id":"C","text":"Improvement in the initial neurological deficit","explanation":"Clinical improvement does not independently clear the patient for antithrombotic therapy."},{"id":"D","text":"Completion of the prescribed alteplase infusion","explanation":"Infusion completion is not sufficient clearance for antithrombotic treatment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Follow post-thrombolysis monitoring/imaging before antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_followup","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_followup"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":15,"correctionRevision":"drug-coverage-20261106","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--21-day","itemId":"verified-alteplase-v35","itemType":"medication","title":"Alteplase (tPA)","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of Alteplase (tPA)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1","explanation":"This describes Antiplatelet. Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"B","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet P2Y12 receptors","explanation":"This describes Antiplatelet. Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Activates plasminogen to break down fibrin","explanation":"Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"This describes Vitamin-K antagonist. Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct fro