 automatically qualifies","explanation":"Later or unknown-onset cases require specific selection criteria."},{"id":"B","text":"The assumption that symptom severity alone establishes eligibility","explanation":"Time, imaging, contraindications, and the protocol remain relevant."},{"id":"C","text":"The assumption that a treatment window excludes imaging requirements","explanation":"Hemorrhage exclusion and eligibility still matter."},{"id":"D","text":"The newest applicable authorized stroke protocol and eligibility assessment","explanation":"The label/guideline distinction must be recognized rather than mixed into an improvised rule."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stroke thrombolysis timing follows current eligibility guidance and local protocol.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_timing"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"A hypothetical 110-kg adult meets the stated stroke protocol eligibility. Using 0.9 mg/kg with a 90-mg maximum, what total dose is calculated? Enter mg.","answer":90,"unit":"mg","tolerance":0,"explanation":"110 × 0.9 = 99 mg before the cap; the maximum limits the total to 90 mg.","point":"Apply the taught 0.9 mg/kg stroke dose with 90 mg maximum.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_total"]},{"stem":"Which BP limits apply immediately before and during the first 24 hours after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Before: <160/90; afterward: ≤160/90 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the protocol limits."},{"id":"B","text":"Before: <185/110; afterward: ≤180/105 mm Hg","explanation":"These are the pre- and post-treatment limits."},{"id":"C","text":"Before: <200/120; afterward: ≤200/120 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the limits."},{"id":"D","text":"Before: <180/105; afterward: ≤185/110 mm Hg","explanation":"This reverses the two thresholds."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distinguish pre-treatment BP eligibility from post-thrombolysis limits.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_bp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_bp"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"An antiplatelet or anticoagulant is proposed after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke. What is required before starting it?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The authorized post-thrombolysis assessment and follow-up imaging plan","explanation":"Bleeding risk and follow-up findings guide the protocol decision."},{"id":"B","text":"A normal platelet count with no visible bleeding","explanation":"These findings do not replace the authorized post-thrombolysis assessment and imaging requirements."},{"id":"C","text":"Improvement in the initial neurological deficit","explanation":"Clinical improvement does not independently clear the patient for antithrombotic therapy."},{"id":"D","text":"Completion of the prescribed alteplase infusion","explanation":"Infusion completion is not sufficient clearance for antithrombotic treatment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Follow post-thrombolysis monitoring/imaging before antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--comprehensive--alteplase_followup","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_followup"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":15,"correctionRevision":"drug-coverage-20261106","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-verified-alteplase-v35","title":"ACTIVASE (ALTEPLASE) KIT [GENENTECH, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=c669f77c-fa48-478b-a14b-80b20a0139c2"},{"id":"aha-stroke-2026","title":"AHA/ASA acute ischemic stroke guideline (2026)","url":"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/STR.0000000000000513"},{"id":"aha-stroke-2026-summary","title":"AHA official 2026 stroke guideline summary","url":"https://professional.heart.org/en/science-news/2026-guideline-for-the-early-management-of-patients-with-acute-ischemic-stroke/top-things-to-know"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA).","mechanism":"Recombinant tissue plasminogen activator that activates fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, producing fibrinolysis.","use":"Emergency thrombolytic used in eligible acute ischemic stroke; current Activase labeling also includes acute myocardial infarction and acute massive pulmonary embolism. Current 2026 AHA/ASA stroke guidance includes alteplase as an IV thrombolytic option for eligible adults within the 4.5-hour treatment window; selected later/unknown-onset cases require advanced-imaging selection.","administration":"IV only and indication-specific. For acute ischemic stroke, current Activase dosing is 0.9 mg/kg (maximum 90 mg), with 10% given as an initial IV bolus and the remainder infused over 60 minutes. MI and massive-PE regimens differ; use the authorized order and current indication-specific protocol. Do not mix other medications into the Activase infusion.","monitor":"• Major risk: serious bleeding, especially intracranial hemorrhage.\n• Before stroke thrombolysis, confirm brain imaging has excluded hemorrhage and verify eligibility/contraindications.\n• Monitor BP and neurological status closely; current stroke protocols use BP <185/110 mm Hg before IV thrombolysis and ≤180/105 mm Hg afterward.\n• Avoid unnecessary invasive procedures and IM injections; inspect puncture sites and other bleeding sites.\n• Sudden severe headache, acute hypertension, nausea/vomiting, or neurological worsening during infusion requires immediate escalation and emergency brain imaging.\n• Follow the stroke protocol for post-thrombolysis monitoring and follow-up imaging before antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy.","why":"Alteplase promotes conversion of fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, breaking down fibrin within a thrombus. That can restore blood flow but also creates the major bleeding risk.","availability":"Local Gaza/Palestinian facility stock has not been independently confirmed. Verify the exact product, strength, order, and local protocol."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA).","id":"section-0-ea898a5b5bddf85c"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Recombinant tissue plasminogen activator that activates fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, producing fibrinolysis.","id":"section-1-f80b8a34bc5297b9"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Emergency thrombolytic used in eligible acute ischemic stroke; current Activase labeling also includes acute myocardial infarction and acute massive pulmonary embolism. Current 2026 AHA/ASA stroke guidance includes alteplase as an IV thrombolytic option for eligible adults within the 4.5-hour treatment window; selected later/unknown-onset cases require advanced-imaging selection.","id":"section-2-6f0c6afc0307aefe"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV only and indication-specific. For acute ischemic stroke, current Activase dosing is 0.9 mg/kg (maximum 90 mg), with 10% given as an initial IV bolus and the remainder infused over 60 minutes. MI and massive-PE regimens differ; use the authorized order and current indication-specific protocol. Do not mix other medications into the Activase infusion.","id":"section-3-eb7d74c96393d2a1"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Major risk: serious bleeding, especially intracranial hemorrhage.\n• Before stroke thrombolysis, confirm brain imaging has excluded hemorrhage and verify eligibility/contraindications.\n• Monitor BP and neurological status closely; current stroke protocols use BP <185/110 mm Hg before IV thrombolysis and ≤180/105 mm Hg afterward.\n• Avoid unnecessary invasive procedures and IM injections; inspect puncture sites and other bleeding sites.\n• Sudden severe headache, acute hypertension, nausea/vomiting, or neurological worsening during infusion requires immediate escalation and emergency brain imaging.\n• Follow the stroke protocol for post-thrombolysis monitoring and follow-up imaging before antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy.","id":"section-4-51417f835785cd03"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Alteplase promotes conversion of fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, breaking down fibrin within a thrombus. That can restore blood flow but also creates the major bleeding risk.","id":"section-5-930a3fafe2c24229"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"Local Gaza/Palestinian facility stock has not been independently confirmed. Verify the exact product, strength, order, and local protocol.","id":"section-6-9fbab64313aa8ea3"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard","itemId":"verified-alteplase-v35","itemType":"medication","title":"Alteplase (tPA)","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of Alteplase (tPA)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bronchodilation with a narrow therapeutic index","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"B","text":"Activates plasminogen to break down fibrin","explanation":"Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Activates receptors to promote glucose uptake","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."},{"id":"D","text":"Binds the alpha-2-delta calcium-channel subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Alteplase promotes fibrin breakdown; it is distinct from preventing new coagulation."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does Alteplase (tPA) belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"LMWH","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA)."},{"id":"B","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA)."},{"id":"C","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA)."},{"id":"D","text":"Unfractionated heparin","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA)."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of Alteplase (tPA)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Indication-specific emergency thrombolysis in eligible patients","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Emergency thrombolytic used in eligible acute ischemic stroke; current Activase labeling also includes acute myocardial infarction and acute massive pulmonary embolism. Current 2026 AHA/ASA stroke guidance includes alteplase as an IV thrombolytic option for eligible adults within the 4.5-hour treatment window; selected later/unknown-onset cases require advanced-imaging selection."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Why must an alteplase prescription identify whether the indication is stroke, MI, or PE?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose and delivery schedule differ by indication","explanation":"The indication-specific regimen must match the authorized order."},{"id":"B","text":"The full vial defines the same dose across indications","explanation":"Container content is not a universal patient dose."},{"id":"C","text":"Body weight alone defines the schedule across indications","explanation":"Weight does not replace the indication-specific regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"The generic drug name defines the schedule without the indication","explanation":"A shared drug name does not establish the same regimen."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Alteplase regimens differ by indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--alteplase_regimen","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_regimen"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For acute ischemic stroke treated with IV alteplase, what percentage of the total dose is given as the initial bolus?","answer":10,"unit":"%","tolerance":0,"explanation":"The regimen gives 10% initially and the remainder by infusion.","point":"The initial stroke bolus is 10% of the total dose.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--alteplase_bolus","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_bolus"]},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For acute ischemic stroke treated with IV alteplase, over how many minutes is the remainder infused after the initial bolus?","answer":60,"unit":"minutes","tolerance":0,"explanation":"The remainder is infused over 60 minutes in the quoted regimen.","point":"The remainder of the taught stroke regimen infuses over 60 minutes.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--alteplase_duration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_duration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which routine procedure should generally be avoided while IV alteplase is being administered for ischemic stroke?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeated neurological checks","explanation":"These are needed for early recognition of neurological deterioration."},{"id":"B","text":"Continuous ECG monitoring","explanation":"Noninvasive rhythm monitoring is not the bleeding-prone procedure."},{"id":"C","text":"Noninvasive blood pressure checks","explanation":"BP reassessment is vital for treatment safety."},{"id":"D","text":"Intramuscular analgesic injection","explanation":"IM injection increases the risk of procedure-related bleeding during thrombolysis."}],"point":"Avoid routine IM injections during IV alteplase to reduce injection-site bleeding.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--alteplase_avoid_im","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_avoid_im"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A second IV medicine is ordered while Activase is infusing. Which administration approach complies with Activase guidance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Mix into Activase after reducing infusion speed","explanation":"A slower rate does not make an unapproved admixture safe."},{"id":"B","text":"Add it to Activase after inspecting for particles","explanation":"Visual inspection cannot establish compatibility with Activase."},{"id":"C","text":"Give through the line without checking the setup","explanation":"Medication line compatibility and the Activase infusion restriction must be respected."},{"id":"D","text":"Administer separately without mixing into Activase","explanation":"Other medications should not be added to an Activase infusion."}],"point":"Do not add another medicine to the Activase infusion.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--standard--alteplase_no_mixing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_no_mixing"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":8},{"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced","itemId":"verified-alteplase-v35","itemType":"medication","title":"Alteplase (tPA)","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"During Alteplase (tPA) therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."},{"id":"D","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::bleeding"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Before alteplase for a suspected ischemic stroke, which completed assessment is essential to the eligibility decision?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal temperature without brain imaging","explanation":"Temperature does not distinguish ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke."},{"id":"B","text":"A low pain score without brain imaging","explanation":"Pain severity does not establish thrombolysis eligibility."},{"id":"C","text":"A normal peripheral pulse without brain imaging","explanation":"Pulse assessment does not exclude intracranial bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Brain imaging excluding hemorrhage with protocol eligibility review","explanation":"Stroke symptoms alone do not establish suitability for thrombolysis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Exclude intracranial hemorrhage and verify eligibility before stroke thrombolysis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--alteplase_screen","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_screen"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During alteplase, sudden severe headache, vomiting, and neurological worsening occur. Which complication is the priority to exclude?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Intracranial hemorrhage","explanation":"This is a major thrombolysis complication requiring immediate escalation and emergency assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"A routine migraine episode","explanation":"The treatment context and acute neurological change require urgent exclusion of hemorrhage rather than assuming a benign headache."},{"id":"C","text":"An uncomplicated therapeutic response","explanation":"Neurological deterioration is not a sign of successful treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"An isolated local infusion reaction","explanation":"A local site problem does not explain this neurological cluster."}],"point":"Acute neurological worsening during thrombolysis needs immediate escalation.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--alteplase_neuro","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_neuro"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"The Activase label states a 3-hour stroke window, while current AHA/ASA guidance supports selected eligible patients within 4.5 hours. What must guide a time-sensitive treatment decision?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The assumption that any later presentation automatically qualifies","explanation":"Later or unknown-onset cases require specific selection criteria."},{"id":"B","text":"The assumption that symptom severity alone establishes eligibility","explanation":"Time, imaging, contraindications, and the protocol remain relevant."},{"id":"C","text":"The assumption that a treatment window excludes imaging requirements","explanation":"Hemorrhage exclusion and eligibility still matter."},{"id":"D","text":"The newest applicable authorized stroke protocol and eligibility assessment","explanation":"The label/guideline distinction must be recognized rather than mixed into an improvised rule."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stroke thrombolysis timing follows current eligibility guidance and local protocol.","criti