c-entry","stem":"A hypothetical 110-kg adult meets the stated stroke protocol eligibility. Using 0.9 mg/kg with a 90-mg maximum, what total dose is calculated? Enter mg.","answer":90,"unit":"mg","tolerance":0,"explanation":"110 × 0.9 = 99 mg before the cap; the maximum limits the total to 90 mg.","point":"Apply the taught 0.9 mg/kg stroke dose with 90 mg maximum.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--7-day--alteplase_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_total"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--alteplase_total"},{"stem":"Which BP limits apply immediately before and during the first 24 hours after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Before: <185/110; afterward: ≤180/105 mm Hg","explanation":"These are the pre- and post-treatment limits."},{"id":"B","text":"Before: <200/120; afterward: ≤200/120 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the limits."},{"id":"C","text":"Before: <160/90; afterward: ≤160/90 mm Hg","explanation":"These are not the protocol limits."},{"id":"D","text":"Before: <180/105; afterward: ≤185/110 mm Hg","explanation":"This reverses the two thresholds."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distinguish pre-treatment BP eligibility from post-thrombolysis limits.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--7-day--alteplase_bp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_bp"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--alteplase_bp","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"An antiplatelet or anticoagulant is proposed after IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke. What is required before starting it?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The authorized post-thrombolysis assessment and follow-up imaging plan","explanation":"Bleeding risk and follow-up findings guide the protocol decision."},{"id":"B","text":"Completion of the infusion as the sole clearance criterion","explanation":"Finishing the dose does not by itself establish that antithrombotics can start."},{"id":"C","text":"Improved speech as the sole clearance criterion","explanation":"Neurological benefit does not replace the protocol’s imaging requirement."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal peripheral pulse as the sole clearance criterion","explanation":"This does not exclude intracranial bleeding."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Follow post-thrombolysis monitoring/imaging before antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-alteplase-v35--7-day--alteplase_followup","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-alteplase-v35::alteplase_followup"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-alteplase-v35--advanced--alteplase_followup","answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":15}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-01-acs","title":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS)","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-lesson-01-acs","itemType":"lesson","title":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS)","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"Which set contains the three presentations of acute coronary syndrome?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"PEA, asystole, ventricular fibrillation","explanation":"These are arrest rhythms rather than ACS categories."},{"id":"B","text":"Heart failure, DCM, pulmonary edema","explanation":"These describe other cardiac diagnoses or manifestations."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable angina, sinus bradycardia, CHB","explanation":"These are not the three ACS categories."},{"id":"D","text":"Unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI","explanation":"These form the ACS spectrum."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"ACS includes unstable angina, NSTEMI, and STEMI.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-feb4be8067a0","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-feb4be8067a0"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which distinction separates unstable angina from myocardial infarction?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"No biomarker evidence of myocardial necrosis","explanation":"Ischemia occurs without the myocardial necrosis defining infarction."},{"id":"B","text":"No possibility of an abnormal ECG","explanation":"Unstable angina can have ischemic ECG changes."},{"id":"C","text":"No coronary blood-flow reduction","explanation":"Coronary ischemia remains central to unstable angina."},{"id":"D","text":"No need for urgent assessment","explanation":"Concerning ischemic symptoms still require urgent assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Unstable angina is ischemia without biomarker evidence of necrosis.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-7092cc8eea66","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-7092cc8eea66"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient has myocardial infarction without persistent ST elevation. Which category best matches?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Complete heart block","explanation":"This is a conduction diagnosis rather than the ACS category."},{"id":"B","text":"NSTEMI","explanation":"This infarction category lacks persistent ST elevation."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable angina","explanation":"This does not describe established myocardial infarction."},{"id":"D","text":"Unstable angina","explanation":"This lacks the biomarker evidence of necrosis defining MI."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"NSTEMI does not require persistent ST elevation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-003ec79aabe2","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-003ec79aabe2"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which presentation most strongly fits the ischemic symptom pattern in ACS?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Brief focal chest discomfort during trunk rotation without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less characteristic of the ischemic pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Sharp localized chest pain reproduced by pressing one rib","explanation":"This is more suggestive of a chest-wall explanation, although symptoms still need assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Central chest pressure with sweating and arm radiation","explanation":"This is the ischemic symptom cluster."},{"id":"D","text":"Burning after a meal with sour regurgitation and no exertional pattern","explanation":"This more closely fits a reflux pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize an ischemic symptom cluster.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-b53b8e091ab1","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-b53b8e091ab1"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"After initial assessment of suspected ACS, which test most directly provides electrical evidence used to guide urgent treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Initial serum electrolyte measurement","explanation":"Electrolytes inform assessment but do not provide a diagnostic electrical tracing."},{"id":"B","text":"Diagnostic 12-lead ECG","explanation":"It supplies the electrical views needed to identify ischemic patterns."},{"id":"C","text":"Continuous pulse-oximeter pleth recording","explanation":"It measures pulsatile flow and oxygenation rather than ischemic electrical views."},{"id":"D","text":"Serial noninvasive BP recording","explanation":"This assesses hemodynamics rather than myocardial electrical patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Initial ACS assessment includes vitals, monitoring, prompt ECG, access, and escalation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-8