ording","explanation":"It measures pulsatile flow and oxygenation rather than ischemic electrical views."},{"id":"D","text":"Serial noninvasive BP recording","explanation":"This assesses hemodynamics rather than myocardial electrical patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Initial ACS assessment includes vitals, monitoring, prompt ECG, access, and escalation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-8ad4d83cf04b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-8ad4d83cf04b"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"What is the immediate physiological problem in ACS?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced coronary supply causing myocardial ischemia","explanation":"This is the central supply problem."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced systemic venous return as the defining mechanism","explanation":"This does not define ACS."},{"id":"C","text":"Primary AV conduction failure without ischemia","explanation":"This defines a different problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Primary ventricular repolarization delay without ischemia","explanation":"This does not define ACS."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Reduced coronary blood flow produces acute myocardial ischemia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-c0b838c1862f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-c0b838c1862f"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient with suspected ACS has adequate oxygenation and no respiratory distress. Which factor should determine supplemental oxygen use?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The presence of ST elevation regardless of oxygenation","explanation":"ST elevation requires urgent care but does not itself establish hypoxemia."},{"id":"B","text":"The severity of pain regardless of oxygenation","explanation":"Pain severity does not itself establish a need for oxygen."},{"id":"C","text":"Evidence of an oxygenation or clinical need under the protocol","explanation":"Oxygen should be given when clinically indicated."},{"id":"D","text":"The ACS diagnosis regardless of oxygenation","explanation":"The diagnosis alone is not the stated indication."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use oxygen when oxygenation or clinical status indicates it.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-5a672f172761","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-5a672f172761"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Chest pressure persists with new ischemic ECG changes and a falling BP. The first troponin result is pending. Which action has priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Escalate immediately while continuing assessment and monitoring","explanation":"Instability and ischemic findings should not wait for a biomarker result."},{"id":"B","text":"Wait for the biomarker result before reporting the ECG","explanation":"This delay can postpone urgent assessment and treatment."},{"id":"C","text":"Complete the dietary history before contacting the team","explanation":"This is secondary to urgent assessment and escalation."},{"id":"D","text":"Schedule a repeat pain score after the routine round","explanation":"This delays action despite deterioration."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Do not delay escalation of concerning ischemia while awaiting biomarkers.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-01-acs--comprehensive--point-dbf4e7e71650","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-01-acs::point-dbf4e7e71650"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient has ongoing ischemic chest pain and compatible new ST elevation. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reason to defer assessment until pain resolves","explanation":"Waiting can delay time-sensitive care."},{"id":"B","text":"A diagnosis of stable exertional angina established by symptoms","explanation":"Persistent acute symptoms and compatible ST elevation require a different response."},{"id":"C","text":"A normal telemetry variant because pain is present","explanation":"Pain does not ma