at all conduct","explanation":"This describes sinus bradycardia rather than complete block."},{"id":"B","text":"A ventricular rate that must exceed the atrial rate","explanation":"This is not the defining relationship."},{"id":"C","text":"Rapid conduction through every atrial impulse","explanation":"This does not describe AV dissociation."},{"id":"D","text":"Failure of atrial impulses to conduct to the ventricles","explanation":"Atria and ventricles then beat independently."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Third-degree AV block produces independent atrial and ventricular activity.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-2ce8ded94a18","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-2ce8ded94a18"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which tracing relationship most supports CHB?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Each P wave followed by one QRS with a fixed PR","explanation":"This demonstrates conducted atrial impulses rather than complete block."},{"id":"B","text":"P waves and QRS complexes with no fixed PR relationship","explanation":"Independent atrial and ventricular rhythms are the hallmark."},{"id":"C","text":"A continuous absence of all atrial and ventricular signals","explanation":"That does not describe an atrial rhythm with a ventricular escape."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal P/QRS relationship with a fast sinus rate","explanation":"This is not complete AV block."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Identify AV dissociation on ECG.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-7fe65b35a1cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-7fe65b35a1cd"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which complication explains dizziness and hypotension during severe CHB?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced cardiac output from a slow ventricular escape","explanation":"Inadequate effective rate can impair perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced platelet aggregation from the escape rhythm","explanation":"The rhythm does not act as an antiplatelet."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased ventilation from atrial dissociation alone","explanation":"This does not explain the hemodynamic problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Increased coronary flow from faster AV conduction","explanation":"Complete block prevents normal AV conduction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A slow escape rhythm can reduce output and organ perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-722bf19cc7c8","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-722bf19cc7c8"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which recent treatment is a plausible contributor to AV conduction slowing in CHB assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A beta-blocker or AV-node-slowing calcium-channel blocker","explanation":"These are named medication contributors."},{"id":"B","text":"A vitamin supplement alone","explanation":"This is not the medication contributor."},{"id":"C","text":"An oral local antifungal alone","explanation":"This is not a principal AV-node-slowing medicine."},{"id":"D","text":"An osmotic laxative alone","explanation":"This is not a direct AV-node-slowing therapy."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review reversible contributors including rate-slowing medicines and electrolyte problems.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-3ec24eb4fcd4","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-3ec24eb4fcd4"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Two patients have a ventricular rate of 38/min. One has fixed P-to-QRS conduction; the other has independent P and QRS rhythms. Which distinction is valid?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A narrow QRS excludes AV dissociation","explanation":"QRS width alone does not establish the P/QRS relationship."},{"id":"B","text":"The identical rates establish the same rhythm diagnosis","explanation":"Rate alone is insufficient."},{"id":"C","text":"A palpable pulse excludes complete block","explanation":"A ventricular escape can 