ast routine meal alone","explanation":"Meal timing does not establish hemodynamic stability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Correlate a conduction pattern with the patient’s perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-ed1c081d8118","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-ed1c081d8118"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient with new CHB has worsening hypoxemia and high potassium. Which interpretation should inform escalation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A palpable escape pulse establishes that both abnormalities are harmless","explanation":"A pulse does not guarantee adequate perfusion or safety."},{"id":"B","text":"Both findings can contribute and need urgent review alongside the block","explanation":"Hypoxia and hyperkalemia are named contributors."},{"id":"C","text":"Neither finding matters once complete block is identified","explanation":"Reversible contributors still require assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"High potassium establishes that oxygenation no longer matters","explanation":"Both physiological problems remain relevant."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Hypoxia and hyperkalemia are reversible contributors to conduction deterioration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--comprehensive--point-8d6be3ffb045","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-02-chb::point-8d6be3ffb045"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8,"model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-lesson-02-chb--7-day","itemId":"ccu-lesson-02-chb","itemType":"lesson","title":"Complete Heart Block (CHB)","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsu