{"id":"aha-bradycardia-2025","title":"AHA adult bradycardia with a pulse algorithm (2025)","url":"https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Bradycardia-250514.pdf?sc_lang=en"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Complete heart block = third-degree AV block. Atrial impulses do not conduct to the ventricles, so atria and ventricles beat independently."},{"label":"ECG hallmark","text":"P waves and QRS complexes march separately with no fixed PR relationship. This is AV dissociation."},{"label":"Why it is dangerous","text":"A slow ventricular escape rhythm can reduce cardiac output and cause dizziness, syncope, hypotension, confusion, ischemia, acute heart failure, shock, or cardiac arrest."},{"label":"Differentiate from sinus bradycardia","text":"Sinus bradycardia: each P wave conducts to a QRS. CHB: P waves and QRS complexes are independent."},{"label":"First nursing actions","text":"• Look at the patient first\n• ABC assessment\n• Check pulse, BP, consciousness, SpO₂, and symptoms\n• Continuous monitoring and 12-lead ECG\n• Establish IV access\n• Escalate rapidly if symptomatic or unstable\n• Prepare for pacing if required"},{"label":"Common causes","text":"• MI/ischemia\n• Beta-blockers\n• Calcium-channel blockers\n• Digoxin\n• Hypoxia\n• Hyperkalemia"},{"label":"Memory","text":"CHB = atria and ventricles beat independently. If perfusion is poor, escalate and prepare for pacing."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Complete heart block = third-degree AV block. Atrial impulses do not conduct to the ventricles, so atria and ventricles beat independently.","id":"section-0-0b9ccc8129707797"},{"label":"ECG hallmark","text":"P waves and QRS complexes march separately with no fixed PR relationship. This is AV dissociation.","id":"section-1-720a1c306a79c25f"},{"label":"Why it is dangerous","text":"A slow ventricular escape rhythm can reduce cardiac output and cause dizzine