","explanation":"This is a different IEC limb-electrode color."},{"id":"D","text":"Red","explanation":"This is a different IEC limb-electrode color."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IEC LL/F color.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--7-day--point-d0658735a25a","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg::point-d0658735a25a"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--standard--point-d0658735a25a","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"In the IEC color system, what color is RL/N?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Green","explanation":"This is a different IEC limb-electrode color."},{"id":"B","text":"Black","explanation":"This is the specified IEC color for RL/N."},{"id":"C","text":"Red","explanation":"This is a different IEC limb-electrode color."},{"id":"D","text":"Yellow","explanation":"This is a different IEC limb-electrode color."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IEC RL/N color.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--7-day--point-7aa70e65b26f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg::point-7aa70e65b26f"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--standard--point-7aa70e65b26f","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"In the IEC limb labels, what do F and N mean?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"F: Foot; N: Neutral","explanation":"These identify the stated foot and neutral labels."},{"id":"B","text":"F: Frequency; N: Node","explanation":"These are not the stated limb label meanings."},{"id":"C","text":"F: Front; N: Neck","explanation":"These are not the stated IEC label meanings."},{"id":"D","text":"F: Filter; N: Negative","explanation":"These are not the stated limb label meanings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"In IEC limb labels F means Foot and N means Neutral.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--7-day--point-ae822decfa7d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg::point-ae822decfa7d"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--standard--point-ae822decfa7d","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"V1 and V2 were placed above the fourth intercostal space, and the tracing now appears unexpectedly abnormal. Which technical issue should be corrected before interpreting a stable patient’s repeat ECG?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The number of electrical leads selected alone","explanation":"A standard lead display does not correct misplaced electrodes."},{"id":"B","text":"The pulse-oximeter’s display brightness","explanation":"That does not correct an ECG electrode-position error."},{"id":"C","text":"Chest-electrode position","explanation":"High V1/V2 placement can distort the tracing."},{"id":"D","text":"The oxygen tubing length alone","explanation":"That does not determine chest-electrode anatomy."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"High V1/V2 placement can produce a misleading tracing.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--7-day--point-4d46dbadc4bf","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg::point-4d46dbadc4bf"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--advanced--point-4d46dbadc4bf","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"V4 is correctly located. What determines the vertical level of V5 and V6?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"One intercostal space below V4","explanation":"This is not the level."},{"id":"B","text":"The same vertical line as V1","explanation":"The sternal V1 position does not set their axillary placement."},{"id":"C","text":"The same horizontal level as V4","explanation":"Their axillary positions share V4’s level."},{"id":"D","text":"One intercostal space above V4","explanation":"This is not the level."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"V5 and V6 share V4’s horizontal level.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--7-day--point-9d000f77a6a7","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg::point-9d000f77a6a7"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-03-12lead-ecg--advanced--point-9d000f77a6a7","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient asks why t