al response check."},{"id":"C","text":"A QRS complex with no temporal relationship to the pacing spike","explanation":"This does not demonstrate a paced response."},{"id":"D","text":"A pacing spike without a following QRS complex","explanation":"A stimulus alone does not establish capture."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Electrical capture is a pacing spike followed by a QRS complex.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--7-day--point-10505ceafd6b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment::point-10505ceafd6b"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--standard--point-10505ceafd6b","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A critically unstable polymorphic VT requires a shock, but reliable synchronization is not possible. Which shock mode is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unsynchronized shock","explanation":"Polymorphic VT cannot reliably support the usual R-wave synchronization."},{"id":"B","text":"Routine pacing to create a marker first","explanation":"This is not the polymorphic VT shock response."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeatedly wait for a stable synchronization marker","explanation":"This can delay needed treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"A maintenance oral antiarrhythmic alone","explanation":"This does not address the immediate electrical emergency."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Polymorphic VT may require unsynchronized shock.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--7-day--point-897c24a18e0f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment::point-897c24a18e0f"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--advanced--point-897c24a18e0f","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Pacing spikes are followed by QRS complexes, but the patient has no corresponding palpable pulse or BP response. What has not been established?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Electrical capture","explanation":"A pacing spike followed by QRS supports electrical capture."},{"id":"B","text":"The presence of ventricular electrical activity","explanation":"QRS complexes establish that electrical finding."},{"id":"C","text":"Mechanical capture","explanation":"Electrical capture alone does not prove effective perfusion."},{"id":"D","text":"The presence of a pacing spike","explanation":"Spikes are visible in the case."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Confirm electrical and mechanical capture.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--7-day--point-c458bf987351","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment::point-c458bf987351"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--advanced--point-c458bf987351","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient has CHB, a very slow pulse, and poor perfusion. Which electrical preparation best matches the physiology?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Shock avoidance based on ECG appearance alone","explanation":"The full clinical state requires urgent treatment preparation."},{"id":"B","text":"Synchronized cardioversion for rapid SVT","explanation":"The rhythm is slow, not a tachyarrhythmia."},{"id":"C","text":"Unsynchronized defibrillation for VF","explanation":"The patient has a pulse and an organized bradycardic rhythm."},{"id":"D","text":"Pacing","explanation":"The problem is dangerous bradycardia with a pulse."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Choose electrical treatment from pulse status, rate, and stability.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--7-day--point-a4b0725b0193","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment::point-a4b0725b0193"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-06-electrical-treatment--advanced--point-a4b0725b0193","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":8}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration","title":"Sudden Deterioration in CCU: What Do You Do First?","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration","itemType":"lesson","title":"Sudden Deterioration in CCU: What Do You Do First?","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"A monitor alarm sounds as a CCU patient deteriorates. What should be assessed first?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The routine medication-round sequence","explanation":"A new deterioration takes priority over routine sequencing."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient’s responsiveness, airway, breathing, and circulation","explanation":"The monitor is interpreted alongside direct clinical assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"The previous shift’s stable observations alone","explanation":"Earlier stability does not establish the current condition."},{"id":"D","text":"The monitor’s diagnostic label without patient assessment","explanation":"A display does not establish the patient’s current physiological state."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the patient and ABCs before relying on the monitor label.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--comprehensive--point-73be98a850e8","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-73be98a850e8"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which observation belongs primarily to the Disability component of an ABCDE assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Respiratory effort","explanation":"This belongs to Breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"Consciousness or new confusion","explanation":"Disability includes neurological state."},{"id":"C","text":"Pulse and skin perfusion","explanation":"These belong to Circulation."},{"id":"D","text":"Airway obstruction","explanation":"This belongs to Airway."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use focused ABCDE assessment to identify immediate problems.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--comprehensive--point-d7b1a704bead","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-d7b1a704bead"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which change is a clear warning of clinical deterioration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New confusion with a marked BP fall","explanation":"Both are red flags for impaired perfusion or another acute problem."},{"id":"B","text":"An unchanged chronic symptom with stable observations","explanation":"No acute warning change is described."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain improving without new symptoms","explanation":"This does not identify acute deterioration."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved breathing with stable BP","explanation":"This describes improvement rather than the warning pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize sudden clinical red flags.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--comprehensive--point-d1f0fd2ce29c","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-d1f0fd2ce29c"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"After an intervention for sudden deterioration, what establishes whether it has helped?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The fact that the intervention was prescribed","explanation":"A correct order does not establish effectiveness in this patient."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient’s observations from the previous day alone","explanation":"These are not a current post-intervention assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeat relevant clinical observations and compare the response","explanation":"Response must be assessed rather than inferred from treatment being given."},{"id":"D","text":"The completion of a medication 