t establishes complete recovery","explanation":"Persistent organ findings contradict that inference."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A BP value must be interpreted with organ perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--comprehensive--point-adbe496f1648","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-adbe496f1648"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":7,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Cardiogenic shock = the heart cannot pump enough blood to perfuse the organs. Think: weak pump → low cardiac output → organ hypoperfusion."},{"label":"Suspect it when","text":"• Hypotension\n• Cold/clammy extremities\n• Weak peripheral pulses\n• Confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Low urine output\n• Tachycardia or significant bradycardia\n• Dyspnea/crackles may coexist"},{"label":"Common CCU causes","text":"• Acute MI\n• Severe heart failure\n• Serious arrhythmia\n• Mechanical complication after MI\n• Severe valve dysfunction"},{"label":"First nursing response","text":"ABC → BP → rhythm → perfusion → escalate. Assess SpO₂/breathing, consciousness, skin, pulses, urine output, and ensure monitoring/IV access."},{"label":"Important mistake","text":"Do not automatically give a large IV fluid bolus. If the pump is failing, excess fluid can worsen pulmonary edema."},{"label":"Cardiogenic vs hypovolemic","text":"Cardiogenic: pump failure; often cold/clammy, weak pulse, possible crackles/pulmonary edema, JVP may be raised. Hypovolemic: empty tank; often cold/clammy, tachycardic, flat neck veins, clear lungs, with bleeding/dehydration/fluid-loss history."},{"label":"Other shock patterns","text":"Distributive shock: vessels are too dilated; septic shock may be warm early. Obstructive shock: flow is blocked, e.g. tension pneumothorax, tamponade, massive PE."},{"label":"Memory","text":"Cardiogenic = weak pump, often wet lungs/full veins. Hypovolemic = empty tank, usually clear lungs/flat veins. Use the whole clinical picture, not one sign."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Cardiogenic shock = the heart cannot pump enough blood to perfuse the organs. Think: weak pump → low cardiac output → organ hypoperfusion.","id":"section-0-66054e0cd72d998e"},{"label":"Suspect it when","text":"• Hypotension\n• Cold/clammy extremities\n• Weak peripheral pulses\n• Confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Low urine output\n• Tachycardia or significant bradycardia\n• Dyspnea/crackles may coexist","id":"section-1-bdd9c7cdad759857"},{"label":"Common CCU causes","text":"• Acute MI\n• Severe heart failure\n• Serious arrhythmia\n• Mechanical complication after MI\n• Severe valve dysfunction","id":"section-2-f40d45e9e623fe08"},{"label":"First nursing response","text":"ABC → BP → rhythm → perfusion → escalate. Assess SpO₂/breathing, consciousness, skin, pulses, urine output, and ensure monitoring/IV access.","id":"section-3-63bf11371a048fe1"},{"label":"Important mistake","text":"Do not automatically give a large IV fluid bolus. If the pump is failing, excess fluid can worsen pulmonary edema.","id":"section-4-82b891c56be94c79"},{"label":"Cardiogenic vs hypovolemic","text":"Cardiogenic: pump failure; often cold/clammy, weak pulse, possible crackles/pulmonary edema, JVP may be raised. Hypovolemic: empty tank; often cold/clammy, tachycardic, flat neck veins, clear lungs, with bleeding/dehydration/fluid-loss history.","id":"section-5-3fe840cd77eaefa3"},{"label":"Other shock patterns","text":"Distributive shock: vessels are too dilated; septic shock may be warm early. Obstructive shock: flow is blocked, e.g. tension pneumothorax, tamponade, massive PE.","id":"section-6-d31341b0cac14dfb"},{"label":"Memory","text":"Cardiogenic = weak pump, often wet lungs/full veins. Hypovolemic = empty tank, usually clear lungs/flat veins. Use the whole clinical picture, not one sign.","id":"section-7-ee24b46940f64013"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard","itemId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock","itemType":"lesson","title":"Cardiogenic Shock & Poor Perfusion","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which mechanism defines cardiogenic shock?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An empty intravascular tank as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes hypovolemic shock."},{"id":"B","text":"Pathological vasodilation as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes a distributive pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Mechanical obstruction to flow as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes an obstructive pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Insufficient cardiac pumping to perfuse organs","explanation":"The primary problem is a weak pump and inadequate effective output."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Cardiogenic shock is pump failure with organ hypoperfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-5fd178011d01","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-5fd178011d01"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which cluster supports concern about inadequate organ perfusion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal pain score with no other findings","explanation":"Pain severity does not establish adequate organ perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Cold skin, weak pulses, confusion, and low urine output","explanation":"These are indicators of hypoperfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Warm comfortable skin with stable mentation alone","explanation":"This does not establish the low-output cluster."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal alertness with improving urine output","explanation":"This is more consistent with improvement."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize a low-perfusion cluster.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-5acab3c6d1db","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-5acab3c6d1db"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which event most directly provides a pump-failure explanation for shock?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A massive pulmonary embolus obstructing forward flow","explanation":"This primarily supports an obstructive mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Major gastrointestinal blood loss with reduced circulating volume","explanation":"This primarily supports a hypovolemic mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute MI with markedly impaired ventricular contraction","explanation":"Myocardial injury can reduce output."},{"id":"D","text":"Severe infection with predominant loss of vascular tone","explanation":"This primarily supports a distributive mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"MI, severe HF, arrhythmia, and mechanical problems can impair the pump.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-daeb54cca273","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-daeb54cca273"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which pair correctly matches a shock type to its mechanism?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Distributive: excessive dilation; obstructive: blocked flow","explanation":"These are the non-pump mechanisms."},{"id":"B","text":"Cardiogenic: empty tank; hypovolemic: blocked flow","explanation":"This reverses the principal definitions."},{"id":"C","text":"Hypovolemic: excessive dilation; cardiogenic: blocked flow","explanation":"This misidentifies both mechanisms."},{"id":"D","text":"Distributive: empty tank; obstructive: weak contraction","explanation":"Those describe different primary mechanisms."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize distributive and obstructive shock mechanisms.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-ae850b96ad8d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-ae850b96ad8d"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock","itemType":"lesson","title":"Cardiogenic Shock & Poor Perfusion","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A hypotensive patient has crackles and suspected pump failure. What is the main concern with assuming a large fluid bolus is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Added fluid reliably distinguishes cardiogenic from hypovolemic shock","explanation":"The whole clinical picture must guide the authorized plan."},{"id":"B","text":"Added fluid directly corrects the loss of myocardial contractility","explanation":"Volume is not an inotrope."},{"id":"C","text":"Added filling load may worsen pulmonary edema","explanation":"A failing pump may not handle the extra volume."},{"id":"D","text":"Added fluid consistently lowers cardiac filling pressure","explanation":"This reverses the immediate loading effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A large automatic fluid bolus may worsen pulmonary edema in pump failure.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-976e3458b01e","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-976e3458b01e"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Patient A has hypotension, raised JVP, and crackles after MI. Patient B has hypotension after major fluid loss, flat neck veins, and clear lungs. Which comparison fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A suggests cardiogenic; B suggests hypovolemic shock","explanation":"The congestion and volume-loss contexts support different mechanisms."},{"id":"B","text":"Both are established as distributive solely from low BP","explanation":"Hypotension alone does not identify the mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"Both are established as obstructive solely from low BP","explanation":"The cases do not provide that common mechanism."},{"id":"D","text":"A suggests hypovolemic; B suggests cardiogenic shock","explanation":"This reverses the principal patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Differentiate pump failure from volume-loss patterns using the full picture.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-a2b5356936cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-a2b5356936cd"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During treatment, BP improves but confusion, cool skin, and oliguria persist. Which conclusion is most defensible?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oliguria establishes successful decongestion by itself","explanation":"Low output does not establish a beneficial treatment response."},{"id":"B","text":"Cool skin excludes ongoing shock","explanation":"Coolness can be a hypoperfusion sign."},{"id":"C","text":"The BP improvement establishes complete recovery","explanation":"Persistent organ findings contradict that inference."},{"id":"D","text":"Perfusion remains concerning despite a better BP","explanation":"Multiple organ indicators must improve before response is assumed."}],"