e is assumed."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A BP value must be interpreted with organ perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--7-day--point-adbe496f1648","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-adbe496f1648"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-adbe496f1648","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":7}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine","title":"Norepinephrine vs Dobutamine","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine","itemType":"lesson","title":"Norepinephrine vs Dobutamine","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"Which primary role best describes norepinephrine in this comparison?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increase vascular tone and support pressure","explanation":"It is primarily a vasopressor."},{"id":"B","text":"Block the gastric proton pump","explanation":"That is an acid-suppression role."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduce platelet aggregation","explanation":"That is an antiplatelet role."},{"id":"D","text":"Increase renal glucose excretion","explanation":"That is an SGLT2-inhibitor role."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Norepinephrine primarily supports vascular tone and pressure.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive--point-34cbee159df1","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-34cbee159df1"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which primary role best describes dobutamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increase cardiac contractility and output","explanation":"It primarily supports the pump."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppress gastric acid through proton-pump blockade","explanation":"This is a PPI action."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduce cardiac contractility through beta1 blockade","explanation":"This is the opposite adrenergic action."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibit platelet P2Y12 receptors","explanation":"This is an antiplatelet action."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Dobutamine primarily supports contractility and output.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive--point-b220fc88d7df","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-b220fc88d7df"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which group best evaluates the response and safety of these vasoactive infusions?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR, tablet count, and diet history alone","explanation":"These do not establish the immediate infusion response."},{"id":"B","text":"Wound length, suture count, and joint range alone","explanation":"These address another clinical domain."},{"id":"C","text":"BP, rhythm, perfusion, and urine output","explanation":"These follow hemodynamic response and potential adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Stool color, bowel frequency, and oral lesions alone","explanation":"These do not assess the principal hemodynamic effects."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor rhythm, pressure, urine output, and peripheral perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive--point-4da335fed1fe","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-4da335fed1fe"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient has both inadequate contractility and dangerously low arterial pressure. Why might the team prescribe both medicines?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They are identical drugs with different trade names","explanation":"They have distinct pharmacological roles."},{"id":"B","text":"They address different components of the hemodynamic problem","explanation":"Dobutamine supports contractility while norepinephrine supports vascular pressure."},{"id":"C","text":"They both work by inhibiting cardiac beta1 receptors","explanation":"Both are not beta1 antagonists."},{"id":"D","text":"They replace the need to monitor clinical perfusion","explanation":"Vasoactive treatment increases the need for monitoring."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pressure and pump support may be combined.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive--point-1455df78142f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-1455df78142f"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A norepinephrine bag is changed to twice the concentration while the required dose is unchanged. What mL/hour change gives the same drug amount?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Four times the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would cause a still larger drug delivery increase."},{"id":"B","text":"Twice the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would deliver four times the prior drug amount."},{"id":"C","text":"The same mL/hour","explanation":"This would double the delivered drug amount."},{"id":"D","text":"Half the previous mL/hour","explanation":"Doubling concentration halves the volume needed for the same dose."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"mL/hour depends on the ordered dose and prepared concentration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--comprehensive--point-7dcb7151eebf","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-7dcb7151eebf"],"answerKey":"D"},{"point":"Convert a specified infusion dose and concentration into mL/hour.","format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For an authorized training-case order, the required dose is 4 micrograms/min and the ready infusion con