em":"A norepinephrine bag is changed to twice the concentration while the required dose is unchanged. What mL/hour change gives the same drug amount?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Twice the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would deliver four times the prior drug amount."},{"id":"B","text":"The same mL/hour","explanation":"This would double the delivered drug amount."},{"id":"C","text":"Half the previous mL/hour","explanation":"Doubling concentration halves the volume needed for the same dose."},{"id":"D","text":"Four times the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would cause a still larger drug delivery increase."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"mL/hour depends on the ordered dose and prepared concentration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--7-day--point-7dcb7151eebf","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-7dcb7151eebf"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-7dcb7151eebf","answerKey":"C"},{"point":"Convert a specified infusion dose and concentration into mL/hour.","format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For an authorized training-case order, the required dose is 4 micrograms/min and the ready infusion concentration is 8 micrograms/mL. What pump rate delivers that dose?","answer":30,"unit":"mL/hour","tolerance":0,"explanation":"4 divided by 8 is 0.5 mL/min; multiply by 60 for 30 mL/hour.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--7-day--point-25a0ccf9688f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-25a0ccf9688f"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-25a0ccf9688f"},{"stem":"BP improves during peripheral norepinephrine, but the IV site becomes painful, pale, and cool. What is the best interpretation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The BP improvement establishes local tissue safety","explanation":"Systemic response does not exclude local ischemia/extravasation."},{"id":"B","text":"A local infusion complication can coexist with systemic pressure benefit","explanation":"Assess and escalate the threatened site promptly."},{"id":"C","text":"Local pallor establishes that the entire circulating volume has fallen","explanation":"The local signs require a site-focused assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Local pain is evidence that the prescribed dose is too low","explanation":"The site change is not a valid reason to increase the infusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Norepinephrine monitoring includes peripheral IV site ischemia/extravasation signs.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--7-day--point-9761561bffe3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-9761561bffe3"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-9761561bffe3","answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":7}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes","title":"Electrolytes That Can Cause Arrhythmias","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes","itemType":"lesson","title":"Electrolytes That Can Cause Arrhythmias","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"Which ECG pattern is compatible with low potassium?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Flattened T waves or U waves","explanation":"These can accompany hypokalemia and ectopy."},{"id":"B","text":"A shortened QT as the main calcium pattern","explanation":"T