xt":"Decongestion may coexist with excessive depletion and renal stress","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude treatment-related harm."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Excess diuresis can cause volume depletion, hypotension, and renal deterioration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--7-day--point-d6f3ea811684","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-d6f3ea811684"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--advanced--point-d6f3ea811684","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":6}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","itemType":"lesson","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"Which two clinical dimensions are used in a practical assessment of heart failure?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Platelet inhibition and clot dissolution","explanation":"These are drug mechanisms rather than HF assessment dimensions."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel obstruction and oral candidiasis","explanation":"These are different clinical problems."},{"id":"C","text":"Drug absorption and tablet dissolution","explanation":"These do not describe the bedside HF pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Congestion and poor perfusion","explanation":"Both may be present and require assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Heart failure can involve congestion, inadequate output, or high filling pressures.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-f62ef09c8257","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-f62ef09c8257"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which cluster most strongly supports congestion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and blurred vision","explanation":"These suggest an antimuscarinic effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech","explanation":"These suggest a different drug-toxicity pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Orthopnea, crackles, edema, and weight gain","explanation":"These are the fluid-backup findings."},{"id":"D","text":"Low urine output, cold skin, and confusion alone","explanation":"These primarily identify poor perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Identify pulmonary/systemic congestion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-f1e56d0ac47b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-f1e56d0ac47b"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which HF finding most directly suggests inadequate organ perfusion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New confusion with cold skin and oliguria","explanation":"This is a low-perfusion cluster."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated peripheral edema without other changes","explanation":"Edema primarily suggests congestion."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated weight gain without other changes","explanation":"Weight gain primarily reflects a volume trend."},{"id":"D","text":"Isolated raised JVP without other changes","explanation":"Raised venous pressure does not alone establish organ perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Identify poor perfusion in HF.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-cc9a37d1c728","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-cc9a37d1c728"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which pairing correctly describes the dominant backup patterns?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Left: GI obstruction; right: urinary obstruction","explanation":"These are different organ problems."},{"id":"B","text":"Left: pulmonary congestion; right: systemic venous congestion","explanation":"Lung and systemic venous findings are distinguished."},{"id":"C","text":"Left: platelet deficiency; right: clotting-factor deficiency","explanation":"These are not HF backup mechanisms."},{"id":"D","text":"Left: systemic venous congestion only; right: pulmonary congestion only","explanation":"This reverses the principal patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Left and right ventricular failure produce different backup patterns.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-6006909ae774","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-6006909ae774"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"What does left ventricular ejection fraction describe?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The percentage of circulating platelets activated","explanation":"This is unrelated to ventricular ejection."},{"id":"B","text":"The percentage of blood oxygenated in the lungs","explanation":"That is not the definition of EF."},{"id":"C","text":"The percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction","explanation":"It is a fractional measure of systolic emptying."},{"id":"D","text":"The percentage of heartbeats conducted through the AV node","explanation":"EF does not measure AV conduction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"EF describes the fraction of LV blood ejected, not the whole clinical severity.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-1309b452709a","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-1309b452709a"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient has a preserved EF but dyspnea and congestion with impaired ventricular filling. Which interpretation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A preserved EF proves all symptoms are noncardiac","explanation":"Other cardiac assessment remains necessary."},{"id":"B","text":"A preserved EF excludes every HF diagnosis","explanation":"Heart failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)."},{"id":"C","text":"A preserved EF establishes normal filling pressures","explanation":"Filling abnormalities may coexist with preserved EF."},{"id":"D","text":"HFpEF can occur with impaired filling despite preserved EF","explanation":"EF alone does not exclude HF or determine severity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Preserved EF does not exclude clinically important HF.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-03676bf960fa","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-03676bf960fa"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient has crackles and edema together with cold skin, hypotension, and confusion. Which classification best captures the bedside picture?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Both congested and poorly perfused","explanation":"The wet and cold patterns can coexist."},{"id":"B","text":"Poorly perfused with congestion excluded","explanation":"Crackles and edema indicate a concurrent congestion concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Congested with adequate perfusion established","explanation":"The hypotension and confusion contradict that assurance."},{"id":"D","text":"Neither congested nor poorly perfused","explanation":"Both sets of findings are present."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Congestion and poor perfusion can coexist.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-539c0d1deede","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-539c0d1deede"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During HF observation, a patient develops sudden severe dyspnea and reduced consciousness. Which assessment has immediate priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The long-term dietary sodium history first","explanation":"This is relevant later but not the first response to acute compromise."},{"id":"B","text":"Airway, breathing, oxygenation, and circulation with urgent help","explanation":"These findings can signal acute cardiopulmonary deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"The planned exercise schedule first","explanation":"Mobilization planning is secondary to acute stabilization."},{"id":"D","text":"The discharge medication supply first","explanation":"This does not address the current emergency."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Acute respiratory or perfusion deterioration in HF needs escalation.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-40564cb2c6cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-40564cb2c6cd"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8,"model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--7-day","itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","itemType":"lesson","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"stem":"Which two clinical dimensions are used in a practical assessment of heart failure?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Platelet inhibition and clot dissolution","explanation":"These are drug mechanisms rather than HF assessment dimensions."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel obstruction and oral candidiasis","explanation":"These are different clinical problems."},{"id":"C","text":"Drug absorption and tablet dissolution","explanation":"These do not describe the bedside HF pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Congestion and poor perfusion","explanation"