pnea and congestion with impaired ventricular filling. Which interpretation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A preserved EF proves all symptoms are noncardiac","explanation":"Other cardiac assessment remains necessary."},{"id":"B","text":"A preserved EF excludes every HF diagnosis","explanation":"Heart failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)."},{"id":"C","text":"A preserved EF establishes normal filling pressures","explanation":"Filling abnormalities may coexist with preserved EF."},{"id":"D","text":"HFpEF can occur with impaired filling despite preserved EF","explanation":"EF alone does not exclude HF or determine severity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Preserved EF does not exclude clinically important HF.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-03676bf960fa","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-03676bf960fa"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient has crackles and edema together with cold skin, hypotension, and confusion. Which classification best captures the bedside picture?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Both congested and poorly perfused","explanation":"The wet and cold patterns can coexist."},{"id":"B","text":"Poorly perfused with congestion excluded","explanation":"Crackles and edema indicate a concurrent congestion concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Congested with adequate perfusion established","explanation":"The hypotension and confusion contradict that assurance."},{"id":"D","text":"Neither congested nor poorly perfused","explanation":"Both sets of findings are present."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Congestion and poor perfusion can coexist.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-539c0d1deede","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-539c0d1deede"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During HF observation, a patient develops sudden severe dyspnea and reduced consciousness. Which assessment has immediate priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The long-term dietary sodium history first","explanation":"This is relevant later but not the first response to acute compromise."},{"id":"B","text":"Airway, breathing, oxygenation, and circulation with urgent help","explanation":"These findings can signal acute cardiopulmonary deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"The planned exercise schedule first","explanation":"Mobilization planning is secondary to acute stabilization."},{"id":"D","text":"The discharge medication supply first","explanation":"This does not address the current emergency."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Acute respiratory or perfusion deterioration in HF needs escalation.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--comprehensive--point-40564cb2c6cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-40564cb2c6cd"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood effectively or requires abnormally high filling pressures. Clinically, think in two directions: congestion and poor perfusion."},{"label":"Congestion","text":"Dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, leg edema, raised JVP, and fluid/weight gain."},{"label":"Poor perfusion","text":"Hypotension, cold/clammy skin, weak pulse, confusion, and low urine output."},{"label":"Left vs right","text":"Left-sided HF mainly backs up into the lungs → dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, pulmonary edema. Right-sided HF mainly backs up into the systemic veins → peripheral edema, raised JVP, abdominal/hepatic congestion."},{"label":"EF","text":"Ejection fraction is the percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction. HFrEF = reduced EF. HFpEF = preserved EF but impaired filling/stiffness. Do not judge severity from EF alone."},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Breathing + BP + rhythm + perfusion + urine output + fluid balance. Escalate sudden severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, falling BP, altered consciousness, dangerous arrhythmia, very low urine output, or ischemic chest pain."},{"label":"Memory","text":"HF patients may be 'wet' from congestion, 'cold' from poor perfusion, or both."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood effectively or requires abnormally high filling pressures. Clinically, think in two directions: congestion and poor perfusion.","id":"section-0-01038e81ba2c45df"},{"label":"Congestion","text":"Dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, leg edema, raised JVP, and fluid/weight gain.","id":"section-1-0aa306d375023543"},{"label":"Poor perfusion","text":"Hypotension, cold/clammy skin, weak pulse, confusion, and low urine output.","id":"section-2-f02440d3d7faa6e1"},{"label":"Left vs right","text":"Left-sided HF mainly backs up into the lungs → dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, pulmonary edema. Right-sided HF mainly backs up into the systemic veins → peripheral edema, raised JVP, abdominal/hepatic congestion.","id":"section-3-098c35a450a36fd1"},{"label":"EF","text":"Ejection fraction is the percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction. HFrEF = reduced EF. HFpEF = preserved EF but impaired filling/stiffness. Do not judge severity from EF alone.","id":"section-4-35937b540387c344"},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Breathing + BP + rhythm + perfusion + urine output + fluid balance. Escalate sudden severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, falling BP, altered consciousness, dangerous arrhythmia, very low urine output, or ischemic chest pain.","id":"section-5-3c16058b74654c22"},{"label":"Memory","text":"HF patients may be 'wet' from congestion, 'cold' from poor perfusion, or both.","id":"section-6-50455106ebb8affc"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard","itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","itemType":"lesson","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","stage":"standard","reviewS