,{"id":"C","text":"The new clinical change","explanation":"Deterioration warrants reassessment and escalation now."},{"id":"D","text":"The routine observation time alone","explanation":"A schedule does not override new warning signs."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Do not normalize a new severe pain trend as ordinary fracture pain.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--comprehensive--point-177f1bb14f6f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-177f1bb14f6f"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":9,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"The one question","text":"With any injured or immobilized limb, ask: Is the tissue below the injury still getting enough blood and nerve supply? Check distal to the injury/cast/fixator and compare with the opposite side when possible."},{"label":"Memory sequence","text":"PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING.\n\n1) PAIN: Is pain suddenly worsening or much worse than expected?\n2) BLOOD: Check color, warmth, capillary refill and distal pulse.\n3) FEELING: Ask about numbness, tingling or burning; compare sensation.\n4) MOVE: Ask the patient to move distal fingers/toes and compare strength/movement.\n5) SWELLING: Look for increasing swelling, a tense limb, or a cast/dressing becoming tight."},{"label":"Compartment syndrome — the emergency","text":"Think: pressure building inside the limb. Trauma/fracture → bleeding and swelling → pressure rises inside a closed muscle compartment → tissue perfusion falls → nerves and muscle can be permanently injured."},{"label":"Early warning to remember","text":"Severe or increasing pain that is out of proportion to the injury, especially pain made worse by passive stretch, is an important warning. A tense swollen compartment and new paresthesia/weakness add concern. Do not wait for every sign to appear."},{"label":"Critical trap","text":"A palpable distal pulse does NOT rule out compartment syndrome. Pallor, paralysis and loss of pulse are late ischemic findings; waiting for the pulse to disappear can delay treatment."},{"label":"Intern responsibilities","text":"Assess distal neurovascular status within your training, recognize deterioration, report findings urgently, document the change and response, and continue reassessment. Check for an obviously constrictive cast/dressing, but do not independently alter prescribed immobilization unless specifically authorized by the local protocol or order."},{"label":"Senior/ordered interventions","text":"Independent treatment decisions, modifying or removing prescribed immobilization/fixation unless specifically authorized, medication changes, compartment-pressure measurement, fasciotomy, and other invasive procedures. Your role is to recognize, report, prepare, and assist within supervision and the authorized MSF/Nasser IBD2 protocol."},{"label":"15-second memory","text":"Fracture patient? Check BELOW the injury: PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING. Severe increasing pain + pain with passive stretch = compartment-syndrome warning. A pulse does not rule it out."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"The one question","text":"With any injured or immobilized limb, ask: Is the tissue below the injury still getting enough blood and nerve supply? Check distal to the injury/cast/fixator and compare with the opposite side when possible.","id":"section-0-a2c5798986110ae0"},{"label":"Memory sequence","text":"PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING.\n\n1) PAIN: Is pain suddenly worsening or much worse than expected?\n2) BLOOD: Check color, warmth, capillary refill and distal pulse.\n3) FEELING: Ask about numbness, tingling or burning; compare sensation.\n4) MOVE: Ask the patient to move distal fingers/toes and compare strength/movement.\n5) SWELLING: Look for increasing swelling, a tense limb, or a cast/dressing becoming tight.","id":"section-1-a042312d7a416752"},{"label":"Compartment syndrome — the emergency","text":"Think: pressure building inside the limb. Trauma/fracture → bleeding and swelling → pressure rises inside a closed muscle compartment → tissue perfusion falls → nerves and muscle can be permanently injured.","id":"section-2-a20f55d5331c88b5"},{"label":"Early warning to remember","text":"Severe or increasing pain that is out of proportion to the injury, especially pain made worse by passive stretch, is an important warning. A tense swollen compartment and new paresthesia/weakness add concern. Do not wait for every sign to appear.","id":"section-3-48a11361f51313a0"},{"label":"Critical trap","text":"A palpable distal pulse does NOT rule out compartment syndrome. Pallor, paralysis and loss of pulse are late ischemic findings; waiting for the pulse to disappear can delay treatment.","id":"section-4-9ff3b1839b5cbabd"},{"label":"Intern responsibilities","text":"Assess distal neurovascular status within your training, recognize deterioration, report findings urgently, document the change and response, and continue reassessment. Check for an obviously constrictive cast/dressing, but do not independently alter prescribed immobilization unless specifically authorized by the local protocol or order.","id":"section-5-2f3272f52ce4a521"},{"label":"Senior/ordered interventions","text":"Independent treatment decisions, modifying or removing prescribed immobilization/fixation unless specifically authorized, medication changes, compartment-pressure measurement, fasciotomy, and other invasive procedures. Your role is to recognize, report, prepare, and assist within supervision and the authorized MSF/Nasser IBD2 protocol.","id":"section-6-dfce8dba2b6fbfda"},{"label":"15-second memory","text":"Fracture patient? Check BELOW the injury: PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING. Severe increasing pain + pain with passive stretch = compartment-syndrome warning. A pulse does not rule it out.","id":"section-7-10371e896c9be3cf"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety","itemType":"lesson","title":"Fracture & Limb Safety — Neurovascular Check","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Where is the key neurovascular assessment directed after a limb fracture or immobilization?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Only at the opposite limb without checking the injured side","explanation":"Comparison cannot replace assessment of the affected limb."},{"id":"B","text":"Only at the frame’s outer metal surface","explanation":"Hardware appearance does not establish tissue perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Only at the uninjured shoulder","explanation":"This does not assess distal tissue at risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Distal to the injury, compared with the other side when possible","explanation":"Tissue below the injury may lose blood flow or nerve function."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess distal blood supply and nerve function below an injury/immobilization.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-4361d515fd95","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-4361d515fd95"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which set best represents a focused neurovascular limb assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain, blood supply, fee