t action."},{"id":"D","text":"Support/protect the limb and report the hardware change","explanation":"Stabilization safety needs authorized assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Loose or broken hardware needs protection and escalation, not independent adjustment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--comprehensive--point-297f9ef846ac","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-297f9ef846ac"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A pin site becomes hot, swollen, and draining. What additional assessment best supports the report?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Vitals, pain trend, wound findings, and distal neurovascular status","explanation":"These establish local and systemic impact."},{"id":"B","text":"The hardware serial number without clinical findings","explanation":"A device identifier does not replace assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"The next elective clinic date without current observations","explanation":"Current deterioration needs current findings."},{"id":"D","text":"The dressing brand without a patient assessment","explanation":"Branding does not describe infection severity or limb function."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the whole patient and wound, report deterioration, document, and reassess.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--comprehensive--point-08afffcbe885","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-08afffcbe885"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":9,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"},{"id":"rnoh-external-fixation","title":"RNOH external fixation/bone correction and lengthening patient guide","url":"https://www.rnoh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-guides/external-fixation-bone-correction-and-lengthening"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"An external fixator stabilizes a fracture using metal pins or screws inserted into bone above and below the fracture and connected to a frame outside the body. It is often used for severe open fractures or when swelling/soft-tissue injury makes immediate internal fixation risky."},{"label":"Build the picture","text":"PINS IN BONE\\nFRAME OUTSIDE","display":"hero"},{"label":"Think first","prompt":"You walk to the bedside. What should you check first: the metal frame or the limb?","text":"The LIMB first. Reuse Lesson 1: pain → blood → feeling → move → swelling. A fixator can look intact while distal circulation or nerve function is worsening.","display":"reveal"},{"label":"Bedside sequence","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT","display":"memory"},{"label":"Scan it in two directions","text":"Visual contrast for bedside review.","display":"split","leftTitle":"LOOK FOR","leftText":"Limb: worsening pain, swelling, neurovascular change.\\nPins/wound: increasing redness, tenderness, drainage, fever.\\nFrame: loose, broken, shifted, or unexpected movement.","rightTitle":"DO NOT","rightText":"Do not independently tighten, loosen, remove, or reposition fixation hardware.\\nDo not invent a pin-cleaning product or schedule; follow the authorized IBD2 order/protocol."},{"label":"LOOSE","text":"Loose/broken/shifted hardware is a protection-and-escalation problem. Support the limb, limit unsafe movement, report it, and do not adjust the fixation system yourself.","display":"stop"},{"label":"Pin / wound care","text":"Pins that enter bone create infection risk. Use strict aseptic technique for pin care, clean/dress the sites according to the authorized health-care/local protocol, and document abnormal findings. Report meaningful changes promptly."},{"label":"Quick decision","prompt":"A pin site is more painful, red and swollen with new drainage. What is your nursing move?","text":"Assess the patient and wound, check temperature/vitals and distal neurovascular status, report the deterioration, document it, and continue reassessment. Follow the local IBD2 pin/wound-care and antimicrobial plan rather than choosing a treatment yourself.","display":"reveal"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. Neurovascular change, infection signs, or a hardware problem = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","display":"memory"}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"An external fixator stabilizes a fracture using metal pins or screws inserted into bone above and below the fracture and connected to a frame outside the body. It is often used for severe open fractures or when swelling/soft-tissue injury makes immediate internal fixation risky.","id":"section-0-d679bdefc5bed92b"},{"label":"Build the picture","text":"PINS IN BONE\\nFRAME OUTSIDE","id":"section-1-889c7721ca32ee2b"},{"label":"Think first","text":"You walk to the bedside. What should you check first: the metal frame or the limb?\n\nThe LIMB first. Reuse Lesson 1: pain → blood → feeling → move → swelling. A fixator can look intact while distal circulation or nerve function is worsening.","id":"section-2-1296a9db59f636fc"},{"label":"Bedside sequence","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT","id":"section-3-86d00a5a53f9149f"},{"label":"Scan it in two directions","text":"Visual contrast for bedside review.\n\nLOOK FOR\n\nLimb: worsening pain, swelling, neurovascular change.\\nPins/wound: increasing redness, tenderness, drainage, fever.\\nFrame: loose, broken, shifted, or unexpected movement.\n\nDO NOT\n\nDo not independently tighten, loosen, remove, or reposition fixation hardware.\\nDo not invent a pin-cleaning product or schedule; follow the authorized IBD2 order/protocol.","id":"section-4-59130a58e7d35a3a"},{"label":"LOOSE","text":"Loose/broken/shifted hardware is a protection-and-escalation problem. Support the limb, limit unsafe movement, report it, and do not adjust the fixation system yourself.","id":"section-5-ebf2136cd38edd54"},{"label":"Pin / wound care","text":"Pins that enter bone create infection risk. Use strict aseptic technique for pin care, clean/dress the sites according to the authorized health-care/local protocol, and document abnormal findings. Report meaningful changes promptly.","id":"section-6-56e3c79b9220e80a"},{"label":"Quick decision","text":"A pin site is more painful, red and swollen with new drainage. What is your nursing move?\n\nAssess the patient and wound, check temperature/vitals and distal neurovascular status, report the deterioration, document it, and continue reassessment. Follow the local IBD2 pin/wound-care and antimicrobial plan rather than choosing a treatment yourself.","id":"section-7-9deca8119d53b79d"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. Neurovascular change, infection signs, or a hardware problem = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","id":"section-8-3ba70f1233025515"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator","itemType":"lesson","title":"External Fixator — Limb, Pins, Frame","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which description best matches an external fixator?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A molded cast enclosing the limb without bone-anchored pins","explanation":"A cast does not have the defining pin-to-frame connection."},{"id":"B","text":"Bone-anchored pins connected to an ext