e frame?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Telescopic strut bodies","explanation":"Struts connect the external rings rather than serving as the skin-crossing bone anchors."},{"id":"B","text":"The ring circumference alone","explanation":"Rings surround the limb; pins/wires provide attachment."},{"id":"C","text":"The software-generated paper schedule","explanation":"Instructions are not a physical bone attachment."},{"id":"D","text":"Wires and/or half-pins","explanation":"They provide the bone-to-frame attachment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pins/wires connect bone to the external rings.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-e57f651c3d2f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-e57f651c3d2f"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"What is a purpose of a Taylor Spatial Frame?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Measurement of compartment pressure during routine walking","explanation":"A TSF is not a compartment-pressure measuring device."},{"id":"B","text":"Gradual deformity correction or limb lengthening","explanation":"These are taught purposes of the frame."},{"id":"C","text":"Direct removal of infected bone without an operation","explanation":"The frame does not itself perform debridement."},{"id":"D","text":"Permanent replacement of an artificial knee joint","explanation":"A TSF is not a joint prosthesis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"TSF may be used for complex fractures, deformity, lengthening, or reconstruction.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-651505cdaed0","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-651505cdaed0"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"What determines planned TSF strut-length changes?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An individualized written correction schedule based on the surgeon’s plan","explanation":"Measurements, imaging, and software support the authorized schedule."},{"id":"B","text":"The intern’s preferred direction of visible alignment","explanation":"Visual preference does not authorize a correction."},{"id":"C","text":"The patient’s pain score as a direct turn-count formula","explanation":"Pain is not an independent adjustment prescription."},{"id":"D","text":"A universal daily turn count used for every TSF","explanation":"The plan is individualized rather than universal."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Correction follows a surgeon-planned individualized schedule.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-d1501cbdc26f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-d1501cbdc26f"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which set best checks limb safety before inspecting the rest of a TSF?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The next scheduled X-ray date without current findings","explanation":"A future study does not replace bedside assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"Ring count and paint condition without a limb examination","explanation":"Device appearance alone cannot establish tissue safety."},{"id":"C","text":"Strut length without a distal examination","explanation":"A length reading does not assess nerve or blood supply."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain trend, perfusion, sensation, movement, swelling, and pressure points","explanation":"These assess tissue and nerve function distal to the injury."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distal neurovascular assessment precedes frame-focused reassurance.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-0cfa2609d327","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-0cfa2609d327"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which TSF change needs prompt reporting?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New purulent drainage or unusual frame movement","explanation":"Infection and hardware changes are both explicit red flags."},{"id":"B","text":"A stable written schedule without new symptoms","explanation":"This does not establish deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"An unchanged pin site with improving comfort","explanation":"This is not the warning pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"A stable alignment without skin pressure or symptoms","explanation":"No new problem is described."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize pin infection, hardware failure, skin pressure, and alignment red flags.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-1445ea942bfc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-1445ea942bfc"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which note best supports reassessment after a TSF concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"“Frame checked; fine” without findings or timing","explanation":"This does not document the actual assessment or response."},{"id":"B","text":"Timed findings, change from baseline, person notified, instructions, and response","explanation":"These describe the event and follow-up objectively."},{"id":"C","text":"“Doctor aware” without the change or received plan","explanation":"The note lacks the information needed for follow-up."},{"id":"D","text":"“Pain expected” without the trend or limb examination","explanation":"This can obscure a clinically important change."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Document time, change, baseline comparison, notification, instructions, and response.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-4bb6b362ceb3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-4bb6b362ceb3"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A trained patient adjusts their own TSF under a written schedule. An intern is asked to change the schedule because the limb looks different. Which distinction matters?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A patient request automatically substitutes for a surgical order","explanation":"A request does not authorize a plan change."},{"id":"B","text":"All TSF adjustments are prohibited even under a patient’s own plan","explanation":"A patient may perform this self-care after individual training and authorization."},{"id":"C","text":"Authorized individual self-care versus a new unsupervised adjustment decision","explanation":"Patient training does not authorize an intern to alter the surgical plan."},{"id":"D","text":"A visible alignment difference provides a numerical correction by itself","explanation":"It does not replace the planned assessment and schedule."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Trained patient self-adjustment does not authorize an intern to improvise.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-d1b91e4aefdd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-d1b91e4aefdd"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A TSF patient has rapidly worsening pain, passive-stretch pain, and new paresthesia while pulses remain present. Which complication is the urgent concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Expected adjustment discomfort established by the pulse","explanation":"The warning cluster needs emergency assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"An isolated cosmetic frame concern","explanation":"New pain and sensory change are tissue-safety findings."},{"id":"C","text":"A normal recovery established by intact hardware","explanation":"Hardware appearance does not establish neurovascular safety."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute compartment syndrome","explanation":"Pulse presence does not exclude threatened compartment perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pulses can persist during early compartment syndrome.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-71d0565ec147","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-71d0565ec147"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A TSF patient wants to increase weight bearing, but the current order is unclear. Which information should determine the activity plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The number of struts as a universal loading rule","explanation":"Strut count does not determine the individual order."},{"id":"B","text":"The verified patient-specific weight-bearing order","explanation":"Device presence alone does not establish permitted loading."},{"id":"C","text":"The patient’s ability to tolerate one unsupported step","explanation":"Tolerance does not replace the structural treatment plan."},{"id":"D","text":"Another TSF patient’s walking allowance","explanation":"Different reconstructions can have different restrictions."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Weight bearing and wound/pin care follow prescribed orders.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-bb61ba030fb4","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-bb61ba030fb4"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A TSF patient’s new sensory change was reported and a management instruction received. Which subsequent action closes the bedside response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Document only the hardware appearance","explanation":"The patient’s neurovascular response remains central."},{"id":"B","text":"Assume notification establishes resolution","explanation":"Notification is not a measured clinical outcome."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeat relevant neurovascular observations and document the response","explanation":"Reporting does not replace ongoing assessment of tissue safety."},{"id":"D","text":"Return to routine checks without comparing the change","explanation":"A new problem requires follow-up of the affected findings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Reassess after a clinical change or intervention.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-573a71c13deb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-573a71c13deb"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Distal pulses remain present, but a TSF contact point is causing new skin breakdown. Which interpretation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A pressure injury still needs assessment and reporting","explanation":"Preserved pulses do not make a local skin injury harmless."},{"id":"B","text":"Skin breakdown authorizes independent frame adjustment","explanation":"The intern must not manipulate hardware independently."},{"id":"C","text":"The absence of fever establishes that no action is needed","explanation":"Pressure injury need not be accompanied by fever."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal pulses establish that the contact pressure is acceptable","explanation":"Neurovascular and skin assessments address different risks."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess and document skin pressure points near the frame.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--comprehensive--point-74f0afc57bfb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-74f0afc57bfb"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":13,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"},{"id":"rnoh-external-fixation","title":"RNOH external fixation/bone correction and lengthening patient guide","url":"https://www.rnoh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-guides/external-fixation-bone-correction-and-lengthening"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"TSF = Taylor Spatial Frame. It is a hexapod circular external fixator used to hold and gradually correct the position of bones. Unlike a simple bar fixator, it can make precise changes in multiple directions."},{"label":"Recognize the parts","text":"Metal rings surround the limb. Wires and/or half-pins pass through the skin into bone, connecting bone to the frame. Six adjustable telescopic struts connect the main rings; complex frames may have additional rings/struts."},{"label":"Why it is used","text":"Selected complex fractures; deformity correction; limb lengthening; difficult or poorly healed fractures (nonunion/malunion), and some limb reconstruction cases. The reason for this patient's TSF depends on the surgeon's plan."},{"label":"How correction works","text":"A surgeon plans the correction using measurements, X-rays, and software. Strut lengths may be changed according to an individualized written schedule. Trained patients/caregivers sometimes make their OWN scheduled adjustments after instruction; an intern must never initiate, change, or improvise a strut adjustment."},{"label":"Assessment: LIMB first","text":"Start distal to the injured limb: pain and trend, colour/warmth, capillary refill, pulses, sensation, toe/finger movement, swelling and skin pressure points. Compare sides when possible. Then assess pin sites, frame integrity, vital signs and overall comfort."},{"label":"Red flag — acute compartment syndrome","text":"Severe or rapidly increasing pain out of proportion (especially on passive stretch), tense swelling, new tingling/numbness or weakness = urgent suspicion. Pulses can remain present early; do not wait for pallor or absent pulse. Escalate immediately for emergency orthopaedic assessment."},{"label":"Pin-site and frame red flags","text":"Report increasing pin-site pain, spreading redness, swelling, new/purulent discharge, fever or feeling unwell. Also report loose/broken wires or struts, unusual frame movement, skin pressure injury or a sudden change in alignment. Do not manipulate hardware yourself."},{"label":"First nursing action & escalation","text":"If abnormal: check patient/vitals and repeat focused neurovascular assessment; protect/support the limb and stop unsafe movement; inform the responsible nurse and orthopaedic team promptly. For suspected compartment syndrome or marked loss of perfusion/sensation/movement, escalate as an emergency. Follow the prescribed wound/pin-care and weight-bearing orders; assist only within supervised intern scope."},{"label":"Reassessment & documentation","text":"Repeat pain and neurovascular observations at the ordered frequency and after a clinical change/intervention. Record time, findings, comparison to baseline, person notified, instructions received and patient response. Recheck pin sites, skin contact points and frame stability visually."},{"label":"Common mistakes","text":"Focusing on the frame instead of the limb; treating worsening pain as 'normal TSF pain'; assuming palpable pulses exclude compartment syndrome; independently turning struts or tightening pins; choosing a pin-cleaning solution or allowing weight-bearing without the local plan."},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"TSF = RINGS + PINS/WIRES + SIX STRUTS. At bedside: LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. New neurovascular problem, infection signs, or broken/loose hardware = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","display":"memory"}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"TSF = Taylor Spatial Frame. It is a hexapod circular external fixator used to hold and gradually correct the position of bones. Unlike a simple bar fixator, it can make precise changes in multiple directions.","id":"section-0-690588ae6e89b2c0"},{"label":"Recognize the parts","text":"Metal rings surround the limb. Wires and/or half-pins pass through the skin into bone, connecting bone to the frame. Six adjustable telescopic struts connect the main rings; complex frames may have additional rings/struts.","id":"section-1-eaca562e9672d6a5"},{"label":"Why it is used","text":"Selected complex fractures; deformity correction; limb lengthening; difficult or poorly healed fractures (nonunion/malunion), and some limb reconstruction cases. The reason for this patient's TSF depends on the surgeon's plan.","id":"section-2-e039187504b4d800"},{"label":"How correction works","text":"A surgeon plans the correction using measurements, X-rays, and software. Strut lengths may be changed according to an individualized written schedule. Trained patients/caregivers sometimes make their OWN scheduled adjustments after instruction; an intern must never initiate, change, or improvise a strut adjustment.","id":"section-3-8ad9980286682f72"},{"label":"Assessment: LIMB first","text":"Start distal to the injured limb: pain and trend, colour/warmth, capillary refill, pulses, sensation, toe/finger movement, swelling and skin pressure points. Compare sides when possible. Then assess pin sites, frame integrity, vital signs and overall comfort.","id":"section-4-3d4f99ec14d14053"},{"label":"Red flag — acute compartment syndrome","text":"Severe or rapidly increasing pain out of proportion (especially on passive stretch), tense swelling, new tingling/numbness or weakness = urgent suspicion. Pulses can remain present early; do not wait for pallor or absent pulse. Escalate immediately for emergency orthopaedic assessment.","id":"section-5-cdd3e613dfd77ccf"},{"label":"Pin-site and frame red flags","text":"Report increasing pin-site pain, spreading redness, swelling, new/purulent discharge, fever or feeling unwell. Also report loose/broken wires or struts, unusual frame movement, skin pressure injury or a sudden change in alignment. Do not manipulate hardware yourself.","id":"section-6-2ee59c59c4a42289"},{"label":"First nursing action & escalation","text":"If abnormal: check patient/vitals and repeat focused neurovascular assessment; protect/support the limb and stop unsafe movement; inform the responsible nurse and orthopaedic team promptly. For suspected compartment syndrome or marked loss of perfusion/sensation/movement, escalate as an emergency. Follow the prescribed wound/pin-care and weight-bearing orders; assist only within supervised intern scope.","id":"section-7-d01bbf9c2cd12be0"},{"label":"Reassessment & documentation","text":"Repeat pain and neurovascular observations at the ordered frequency and after a clinical change/intervention. Record time, findings, comparison to baseline, person notified, instructions received and patient response. Recheck pin sites, skin contact points and frame stability visually.","id":"section-8-bf1b53b681cf7298"},{"label":"Common mistakes","text":"Focusing on the frame instead of the limb; treating worsening pain as 'normal TSF pain'; assuming palpable pulses exclude compartment syndrome; independently turning struts or tightening pins; choosing a pin-cleaning solution or allowing weight-bearing without the local plan.","id":"section-9-7a44e591801c34e8"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"TSF = RINGS + PINS/WIRES + SIX STRUTS. At bedside: LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. New neurovascular problem, infection signs, or broken/loose hardware = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","id":"section-10-0fa1b825b8464dac"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard","itemId":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame","itemType":"lesson","title":"Taylor Spatial Frame (TSF) — Bedside Nursing","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"What does Taylor Spatial Frame describe?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A circular external fixator with adjustable struts","explanation":"This describes the TSF system."},{"id":"B","text":"A skeletal traction system with suspended weights","explanation":"Traction weights are not the TSF system."},{"id":"C","text":"A linear external fixator with a single connecting bar","explanation":"This lacks the characteristic circular hexapod arrangement."},{"id":"D","text":"An internal fixation plate with locking screws","e